// v3/spec.dag — Minimal compiler specification.
//
// This file IS the spec. It defines every type the v3 compiler operates on,
// building up from first principles to pipeline contracts. Each layer is
// derived from prior layers. Nothing is axiomatic that can be derived.
// Nothing is named before it emerges from structure.
//
// THESIS (from THESIS.md): gunbc is a causal engine. Its job is to validate
// that a program has a consistent cause-and-effect flow from source to drain.
// A .dag program IS a dependency graph. All data is finite. All iteration is
// bounded. Correctness dimensions (termination, ownership, effects, user-defined)
// are lattice values carried on edges. If the proof constructs, emission is
// mechanical translation.
//
// Layers (each derived from prior layers):
//
//   0  Truth + Finiteness    — the primitive and the axiom
//   1  Function              — the sole computation primitive (implication)
//   2  Type structure        — emerges from Classical + Function
//   3  Self-reference        — observable graph property, not a declared type
//   4  Observed patterns     — BoundedLattice, Monoid (named regularities)
//   5  The DAG IR            — Node, Edge (two designs explored)
//   6  Edge vocabulary       — SubValueRelation, UsageEdge
//   7  Dimension mechanism   — Dimension<T>, built-in dimensions
//   8  Proof strategies      — fold + lattice + gate
//   9  Pipeline contracts    — parse / resolve / prove / emit

module v3.spec


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 0: TRUTH + FINITENESS
//
// The single primitive and the single axiom. Everything else derives.
//
// Classical: a proposition holds or doesn't. The smallest unambiguous
// unit of meaning in digital computing. Bit, Bool, and all integers
// are composed from this.
//
// Finiteness: all data is representable in a finite number of bits.
// The largest value is Word64. There is no infinity. This makes every
// property decidable, every computation bounded, every proof constructive.
//
// Together: truth + finiteness = a closed system. This is what makes
// the causal engine possible.
// =========================================================================

type Classical = True | False

data max_inhabitants: Int = 9223372036854775807


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 1: FUNCTION
//
// The sole computation primitive. A → B: "given A, produce B."
//
// Function is implication. It is the directed edge in the causal graph.
// Without it, there is no causation, no computation, no dependencies.
// The program IS a graph of function applications.
//
// Function is a language primitive — it exists before any type can be
// defined, because type definitions themselves use functions (a
// constructor IS a function from field values to the constructed type).
//
// The syntax:
//   fn(A) -> B       (named function)
//   (a) => expr      (anonymous function / lambda)
// =========================================================================

// (Function is built-in, not a declared type. This layer exists to mark
// its position in the derivation: after truth, before type structure.)


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 2: TYPE STRUCTURE
//
// Given Classical (Layer 0) and Function (Layer 1), type structure
// EMERGES. We don't declare Product and Coproduct as opaque axioms —
// we observe how they arise from combining truth values.
//
// A type definition says: "here are the values that inhabit this type."
// There are exactly three structural shapes a type definition can take,
// corresponding to the three ways truth values compose:
//
// PRODUCT (conjunction, ×):
//   type Pair { first: A, second: B }
//   "first holds AND second holds." All fields are present.
//   The cardinality: |Pair| = |A| · |B|
//   The compiler SEES this shape when a type has named fields.
//
// COPRODUCT (disjunction, +):
//   type Either = Left { value: A } | Right { value: B }
//   "Left holds OR Right holds." Exactly one variant is selected.
//   The tag that identifies which variant is active is a bounded
//   selector composed from Classical bits — log2(N) bits for N variants.
//   Classical itself is the simplest coproduct: 2 variants, 1-bit tag.
//   The cardinality: |Either| = |A| + |B|
//   The compiler SEES this shape when a type has variants separated by |.
//
// FUNCTION (implication, →):
//   fn(A) -> B
//   "Given A, produce B." A mapping from inputs to outputs.
//   The cardinality: |A → B| = |B|^|A|
//   Already defined in Layer 1.
//
// These are NOT separate types to declare. They are structural shapes
// that the compiler recognizes in type definitions:
//   { fields }     → product (all hold)
//   = V1 | V2 | V3 → coproduct (one holds, tagged)
//   fn(A) -> B     → function (mapping)
//
// The boundary cases complete the arithmetic:
//
// UNIT (1): the product of zero fields. One inhabitant (the empty record).
//   type Unit {}
//   Identity for product: A × 1 = A.
//   In logic: trivial truth (⊤). In set theory: singleton set {∗}.
//
// VOID (0): the coproduct of zero variants. Zero inhabitants.
//   type Void = (nothing — no variants)
//   Identity for coproduct: A + 0 = A.
//   Annihilator for product: A × 0 = 0.
//   In logic: absurdity (⊥). In set theory: empty set ∅.
//
// OPTIONAL: not a separate concept. A + Unit = A + 1.
//   type Maybe<A> = Present { value: A } | Absent
//   "Either A is present, or it's absent (Unit)."
//   The T? syntax is sugar for this coproduct.
//
// WHY THIS MATTERS for emission: product/coproduct/function are
// cardinality arithmetic (×, +, ^). Every target has arithmetic:
//   Verilog gates = AND/OR. SQL = JOIN/UNION. REST = request/alternatives.
//   SPICE = parallel/switched paths. The structure is universal.
//
// The compiler doesn't need `type Product` or `type Coproduct` as
// declared types. It reads the shape from the syntax of each type
// definition. The shape IS the definition.
// =========================================================================

// (No types declared here — product, coproduct, unit, void, optional
// are structural shapes that the compiler recognizes, not types to define.
// Classical from Layer 0 is itself the simplest coproduct: True + False.)


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 3: SELF-REFERENCE
//
// Types can reference themselves. Functions can call themselves.
// These are structural facts in the definition graph — CYCLES.
// The compiler detects them by walking the graph. No separate types
// needed to name sub-categories.
//
// SELF-REFERENTIAL TYPES:
//   type List<T> = Cons { head: T, tail: List<T> } | Nil
//   The field `tail` has type `List<T>` — the same type being defined.
//   The compiler sees the cycle in the type definition graph.
//   All instances are finite (closure axiom: max_inhabitants elements).
//
// SELF-CALLING FUNCTIONS:
//   fn length(xs: List<T>) -> Int {
//     match xs { Nil => 0  Cons { head: _, tail: rest } => 1 + length(rest) }
//   }
//   The function `length` calls `length`. The compiler sees the cycle
//   in the call graph.
//
// The compiler observes HOW the recursive argument changes at each
// self-call. This is not a type to declare — it is structural analysis
// of the call expression:
//
//   length(rest) where rest = xs.tail
//     → argument is a FIELD of the input → structurally smaller
//
//   sum(skip(1, xs))
//     → argument is input with one element removed → collection shrink
//
//   factorial(n - 1)
//     → argument is input reduced by arithmetic → arithmetic descent
//
//   loop(same_arg)
//     → argument unchanged → bounded by max_inhabitants (the closure axiom)
//
// The compiler doesn't need `RecursionShape` or `IterationPrimitive`
// enums. It reads the call expression, compares the argument to the
// parameter, and classifies the relationship. That classification IS
// the SubValueRelation (Layer 6) — the same type used for all edges.
//
// Similarly, the RATE of decrease (by 1, by k, by factor n/k) is
// observable from the call expression:
//   skip(1, xs)  → decrease by 1
//   skip(k, xs)  → decrease by k
//   n / 2        → decrease by factor 2
// The compiler reads the literal or expression and records the rate.
// No `ShrinkFactor` type needed until the compiler needs to represent
// this observation internally (Layer 6, as part of SubValueRelation).
// =========================================================================

// (No types declared here. Self-reference and recursion are observable
// graph properties. The compiler detects cycles and classifies how
// arguments change. The classification vocabulary is SubValueRelation,
// defined in Layer 6 where it serves as the edge vocabulary.)


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 4: OBSERVED PATTERNS (algebraic structures)
//
// Given Layers 0-3, certain patterns recur across many concrete types.
// These are not primitives — they are REGULARITIES that the compiler
// discovers. Naming them lets the compiler reason generically:
// "this operation is associative with an identity" enables fold
// optimization, parallel reduction, and algebraic simplification —
// regardless of what the specific type is.
//
// BoundedLattice is the pattern most critical to the compiler:
// every correctness dimension exhibits lattice structure. But the
// lattice is not a foundation — it is something the compiler DISCOVERS
// in std/ declarations. User-defined dimensions that declare a lattice
// get the same treatment as built-in ones. No special compiler support.
// =========================================================================

// BoundedLattice: the pattern for merging at join points.
// Any finite partial order has meets and joins (finiteness guarantees this).
// The lattice structure is a CONSEQUENCE of finiteness + order.
type BoundedLattice<T> {
  bottom: T
  top: T
  meet: fn(T, T) -> T
  join: fn(T, T) -> T
}

// Monoid: the pattern for sequential composition.
// "This operation is associative with an identity element."
// Enables algebraic simplification: (A then B) then C = A then (B then C).
type Monoid<T> {
  identity: T
  op: fn(T, T) -> T
}

// Ordering: three-valued comparison.
// The total order on natural numbers (Layer 0 + finiteness).
type Ordering = Less | Equal | Greater


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 5: THE DAG IR
//
// The graph substrate. A .dag program IS a dependency graph. The IR must
// make every dependency an explicit edge, every binding an explicit site
// with structural identity, and every value relationship classifiable.
//
// v2's Node has 18 fields — most unused on most nodes. The IR carries
// compiler state (is_self_recursive, has_non_tail_self_call), parallel
// representations (connective vs Product/Coproduct, VarBindingKind vs
// edge position), and stringly-typed identity (Node.name). This layer
// explores two clean-room alternatives.
//
// SHARED PRIMITIVES (both designs use these):
// =========================================================================

// Structural identity. Integer, not string. No Node.name field.
// The InternTable maps names to ids at parse time; downstream stages
// never touch strings for identity.
type NodeId = Int

// Source location for diagnostics. Every node knows where it came from.
type Span { file: String, start: Int, end: Int }

// Literal values — the leaves of computation.
type LiteralValue
  = LitInt { value: Int }
  | LitFloat { value: Float }
  | LitString { value: String }
  | LitBool { value: Bool }

// Binary operators — algebraic operations on values.
type BinOp
  = Add | Sub | Mul | Div | Mod
  | Eq | Neq | Lt | Gt | Lte | Gte
  | And | Or
  | Concat

// Reference to a binding. Structural, not stringly-typed.
// The NodeId points to the binding site (parameter, let, match arm).
// Scope resolution produces these from parsed names.
type BindingRef { id: NodeId }

// Reference to a field within a product type.
type FieldRef { type_id: NodeId, field_name: String }

// Reference to a function parameter by position (not name).
type ParamRef { func_id: NodeId, index: Int }


// =========================================================================
// DESIGN A: LEAN UNIVERSAL NODE (Layer 5a)
//
// One node type. All structural relationships are edges. Uniform traversal
// is trivial: walk node.edges. The NodeKind discriminator carries only
// per-kind data (no shared unused fields).
//
// Tradeoff: edge labels must encode structural roles ("which child is the
// scrutinee?"). The node itself doesn't know — the edge knows.
//
// This is the v2 philosophy (universal carrier) cleaned up: 4 fields
// instead of 18, edges instead of bare child lists, integer identity
// instead of string names.
// =========================================================================

type UnivNode {
  id: NodeId
  kind: NodeKind
  span: Span
  edges: List<UnivEdge>
}

// Edges in the universal design carry a structural role label.
// The label says "I am the scrutinee of a match" or "I am the 2nd argument."
// SVR (Layer 3) lives here too — every edge classifies the value relationship.
type UnivEdge {
  role: EdgeRole
  target: NodeId
}

// What role does this child play in its parent?
// Finite enum — one variant per structural position across all node kinds.
// This replaces v2's params/body/children/transport/properties split.
type EdgeRole
  = Child { index: Int }       // positional child (e.g., list elements)
  | Param { index: Int }       // function/lambda parameter declaration
  | Arg { index: Int }         // call-site argument value
  | Body                       // function/lambda/let body
  | Scrutinee                  // match/if condition
  | Arm { index: Int }         // match arm (pattern + body)
  | ArmPattern                 // pattern within a match arm
  | ArmBody                    // body within a match arm
  | TypeAnnotation             // declared type on a binding
  | Receiver                   // method call receiver
  | Init                       // initial value (fold accumulator, let value)
  | Collection                 // collection being iterated (fold, for-each)
  | FieldTarget                // target of field access
  | Left                       // left operand of binary op
  | Right                      // right operand of binary op
  | Operand                    // unary op operand
  | Element { index: Int }     // record/list literal element
  | ImportTarget               // module being imported
  | Transport                  // external execution strategy

// What kind of node is this? Each variant carries ONLY its specific data.
// No shared fields — those live on UnivNode itself (id, span, edges).
type NodeKind
  // --- Declarations ---
  = ModuleDecl { name: String }
  | ImportDecl { path: String, names: List<String> }
  | TypeDecl { name: String }
  | FuncDecl { name: String, is_async: Bool }
  | DataDecl { name: String }
  | ServiceDecl { name: String }
  // --- Expressions ---
  | Literal { value: LiteralValue }
  | Var { ref: BindingRef }
  | Call
  | MethodCall { method: String }
  | Match
  | If
  | Let { name: String }
  | Lambda
  | Block
  | FieldAccess { field: String }
  | BinaryOp { op: BinOp }
  | UnaryOp { op: UnaryOpKind }
  | RecordLit { type_name: String }
  | ListLit
  | StringInterp
  | ForEach { binding: String }
  | Index
  | Return
  // --- Structural ---
  | ParamNode { name: String }
  | ArgNode { name: String }
  | ArmNode
  | FieldInitNode { name: String }
  | VariantDecl { name: String }
  | FieldDecl { name: String }

type UnaryOpKind = Not | Neg

// No TypeShape enum. Whether a type is product or coproduct is visible
// from its definition: fields → product, variants (|) → coproduct.
// The compiler reads the children of a TypeDecl node to determine this.


// =========================================================================
// DESIGN B: TYPED NODES (Layer 5b)
//
// Each syntactic form is its own type. No unused fields. Precise types
// enable exhaustive pattern matching on the IR without runtime checks.
//
// Tradeoff: uniform traversal requires either (a) a top-level Expr
// coproduct that wraps everything, or (b) a generic children_of function
// per type. Pattern matching is exhaustive but verbose.
//
// This is the "typed AST" philosophy used by most production compilers
// (Rust's HIR, TypeScript's AST, etc.).
// =========================================================================

// Top-level declaration — what a module contains.
type Decl
  = DeclType { name: String, fields: List<TypedFieldDecl>, variants: List<Decl>, span: Span }
  | DeclFunc { name: String, params: List<TypedParam>, body: Expr, return_type: TypeRef?, is_async: Bool, span: Span }
  | DeclData { name: String, body: Expr, span: Span }
  | DeclService { name: String, operations: List<Decl>, span: Span }
  | DeclImport { path: String, names: List<String>, span: Span }

type TypedFieldDecl { name: String, type_ref: TypeRef, span: Span }
type TypedParam { name: String, type_ref: TypeRef, span: Span }

// Type references — what appears in type annotations (surface syntax).
// OptionalType is sugar: T? = T + Unit (coproduct with unit).
// The resolve stage lowers it to a named coproduct.
type TypeRef
  = NamedType { name: String, args: List<TypeRef> }
  | FuncType { params: List<TypeRef>, result: TypeRef }
  | OptionalType { inner: TypeRef }

// Expressions — the computation.
type Expr
  = ExprLiteral { value: LiteralValue, span: Span }
  | ExprVar { ref: BindingRef, span: Span }
  | ExprCall { func: Expr, args: List<CallArg>, span: Span }
  | ExprMethodCall { receiver: Expr, method: String, args: List<CallArg>, span: Span }
  | ExprMatch { scrutinee: Expr, arms: List<MatchArm>, span: Span }
  | ExprIf { condition: Expr, then_branch: Expr, else_branch: Expr?, span: Span }
  | ExprLet { name: String, type_ann: TypeRef?, value: Expr, span: Span }
  | ExprLambda { params: List<TypedParam>, body: Expr, span: Span }
  | ExprBlock { stmts: List<Expr>, span: Span }
  | ExprFieldAccess { target: Expr, field: String, span: Span }
  | ExprBinOp { op: BinOp, left: Expr, right: Expr, span: Span }
  | ExprUnaryOp { op: UnaryOpKind, operand: Expr, span: Span }
  | ExprRecordLit { type_name: String, fields: List<FieldInit>, span: Span }
  | ExprListLit { elements: List<Expr>, span: Span }
  | ExprStringInterp { parts: List<StringPart>, span: Span }
  | ExprForEach { binding: String, collection: Expr, body: Expr, span: Span }
  | ExprIndex { target: Expr, index: Expr, span: Span }
  | ExprReturn { value: Expr?, span: Span }

type CallArg { name: String?, value: Expr }
type FieldInit { name: String, value: Expr }
type StringPart = TextPart { text: String } | InterpPart { expr: Expr }

type MatchArm { pattern: Pattern, guard: Expr?, body: Expr, span: Span }

type Pattern
  = PatternBind { name: String }
  | PatternLiteral { value: LiteralValue }
  | PatternVariant { name: String, parent: String?, bindings: List<FieldPattern> }
  | PatternWildcard

type FieldPattern { name: String, binding: String }


// =========================================================================
// DESIGN COMPARISON
//
// Cost of adding a new expression kind:
//   Design A: 1 NodeKind variant + its EdgeRole usage (if new roles needed)
//   Design B: 1 Expr variant + update children_of traversal function
//
// Cost of uniform traversal:
//   Design A: walk node.edges — O(1) code, works for any node kind
//   Design B: match on Expr — one arm per variant, must be exhaustive
//
// Cost of "which child is the scrutinee?":
//   Design A: find edge with role == Scrutinee — O(edges) lookup
//   Design B: expr.scrutinee — O(1) field access, type-checked
//
// Cost of dimension attachment:
//   Design A: dimension values on UnivEdge — uniform, every edge has them
//   Design B: dimension values in side tables keyed by NodeId — separate
//
// Recommendation for v3: Design A for the compiler IR (uniform traversal
// is critical for the generic proof mechanism), with Design B's type
// precision used for the SURFACE AST that the parser produces. The
// resolve stage lowers Design B → Design A. This gives both:
//   - Precise, exhaustive parsing (Design B)
//   - Uniform proof/dimension/emit traversal (Design A)
// =========================================================================


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 6: EDGE VOCABULARY
//
// How a target value relates to a source value along an edge. This is
// the key insight from the v2 branch analysis: SubValueRelation already
// IS the edge vocabulary. It classifies every dependency relationship
// in the program. The compiler's job is to attach an SVR to every edge
// and then read it downstream — no reconstruction, no heuristics.
//
// Authority: induction.dag (std/), binding-model-proposal.md
// Depends on: Layer 5 (NodeId, FieldRef, ParamRef)
// =========================================================================

// ShrinkFactor: the rate of decrease the compiler observes at a
// self-call. Distinguishes O(n) from O(log n). This is the compiler's
// internal representation of what it reads from call expressions
// (Layer 3: skip(1) → UnitShrink, n/2 → ProportionalShrink).
type ShrinkFactor
  = UnitShrink                           // decrease by 1
  | ConstantShrink { amount: Int }       // decrease by k
  | ProportionalShrink { divisor: Int }  // decrease by factor n/k

// SubValueRelation: how the target value relates to the source value
// along an edge. This IS the provenance dimension AND the structural
// edge classifier. Already a BoundedLattice (meet/join defined).
//
// Every edge in the DAG carries one of these:
//   StrictSubValue:      target is structurally smaller (field of source)
//   IteratedSubValue:    target is an element of a collection in source
//   ArithmeticDescent:   target is source reduced by arithmetic (n-1, n/2)
//   PreservedValue:      target IS source (passed through unchanged)
//   Unknown:             relationship not determined (fail-closed to bottom)
type SubValueRelation
  = StrictSubValue { field: FieldRef, factor: ShrinkFactor }
  | IteratedSubValue { field: FieldRef }
  | ArithmeticDescent { param: ParamRef, factor: ShrinkFactor }
  | PreservedValue
  | Unknown

// SVR lattice operations. Bottom = Unknown (fail-closed: no information).
// Top = StrictSubValue (strongest: provably smaller).
// meet = conservative merge (weaker wins at branch join points).
fn meet_svr(a: SubValueRelation, b: SubValueRelation) -> SubValueRelation {
  match a {
    Unknown => Unknown
    PreservedValue => match b {
      StrictSubValue { field: _, factor: _ } => PreservedValue
      IteratedSubValue { field: _ } => PreservedValue
      ArithmeticDescent { param: _, factor: _ } => PreservedValue
      PreservedValue => PreservedValue
      Unknown => Unknown
    }
    _ => match b {
      Unknown => Unknown
      PreservedValue => PreservedValue
      _ => a
    }
  }
}

// SVR composition: if A →svr1→ B →svr2→ C, what is A → C?
// StrictSubValue composes: field of field is still strictly sub.
// IteratedSubValue of StrictSubValue: element of a field — still sub.
// Anything composed with Unknown → Unknown.
// Anything composed with PreservedValue → unchanged.
fn compose_svr(outer: SubValueRelation, inner: SubValueRelation) -> SubValueRelation {
  match outer {
    Unknown => Unknown
    PreservedValue => inner
    _ => match inner {
      Unknown => Unknown
      PreservedValue => outer
      _ => inner
    }
  }
}


// UsageEdge: what happens to the value at each use site.
// Orthogonal to SVR (which describes the relationship along the edge,
// not what the consumer does with the value).
//
// This is the ONLY genuinely new type identified in the v2 vocabulary
// reconciliation. Everything else collapsed into SVR.
type UsageEdge
  = Consumed     // moved / ownership transferred (last use candidate)
  | Read         // read but not consumed (borrow candidate)
  | Projected    // accessed via field / index (structural borrow)
  | Threaded     // passed through unchanged (fold accumulator)


// =========================================================================
// The SVR-keyed dimension table.
//
// Every correctness dimension computes a value from the SubValueRelation
// on the binding's edge. This table is the architectural bet: one row
// per SVR variant, one column per dimension.
//
// Adding a dimension = adding a column.
// Adding a value relationship = adding a row.
// Cost of change: O(1).
//
// The boundary-crossing question ("is this a parameter?") determines
// WHO provides the SVR, not what the SVR is:
//   Parameter:   caller provides SVR (read from call site)
//   Let-binding: derive SVR from the expression
//
// This replaces v2's triple/quadruple classification system:
//   classify_binding_provenance, classify_let_value,
//   classify_argument, classify_body_provenance,
//   classify_self_call_evidence — all deleted.
// =========================================================================

// The table itself is conceptual — each dimension defines its own
// mapping from SVR → dimension value. But the structure is uniform:
//
//   | SVR                | Provenance       | Ownership | Descent        |
//   |--------------------|------------------|-----------|----------------|
//   | StrictSubValue     | StrictSubValue   | Borrowed  | Strict         |
//   | IteratedSubValue   | IteratedSubValue | Owned     | Strict         |
//   | ArithmeticDescent  | ArithDescent     | Owned     | Strict         |
//   | PreservedValue     | PreservedValue   | source    | NonIncreasing  |
//   | Unknown            | Unknown          | Shared    | DescentUnknown |
//
// The "source" entry for PreservedValue ownership means: inherit from
// the source binding. If the source is Owned, the preserved value is
// Owned. If Borrowed, Borrowed. PreservedValue is identity for ownership.

// Iteration contracts: which SVR each bounded primitive provides.
// fold/descend/repeat are not special types — they're call sites that
// provide specific SubValueRelations to their body parameters.
//
//   | Primitive                              | Param   | SVR provided     |
//   |----------------------------------------|---------|------------------|
//   | fold(coll, init, (acc, elem) => ...)   | acc     | PreservedValue   |
//   | fold(coll, init, (acc, elem) => ...)   | elem    | IteratedSubValue |
//   | descend(node, (child) => ...)          | child   | StrictSubValue   |
//   | repeat(n, init, (i, acc) => ...)       | acc     | PreservedValue   |
//   | repeat(n, init, (i, acc) => ...)       | i       | Unknown          |
//
// No IterationBinding type needed. The contract is: "what SVR does this
// call site attach to each parameter edge?"


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 7: DIMENSION MECHANISM
//
// The generic DECLARE / COMPUTE / CARRY / ENFORCE pattern.
//
// Every correctness dimension — built-in or user-defined — follows the
// same architecture. The compiler doesn't have "a complexity pass" and
// "an ownership pass." It has ONE mechanism that reads whatever dimensions
// std/ declares and enforces them all uniformly.
//
// Adding a dimension = declaring a lattice in std/ + its binding-site rule.
// The compiler carries it generically. Cost of change: one file.
//
// Authority: dimensions-design.md (src/v2/), THESIS.md §Correctness dimensions
// Depends on: Layer 4 (BoundedLattice), Layer 6 (SVR, UsageEdge)
// =========================================================================

// The dimension contract. Any type T that satisfies this contract can be
// carried through the compiler as a correctness dimension.
//
// 1. DECLARE: the lattice (vocabulary)
// 2. COMPUTE: at each binding site (producer rule)
// 3. CARRY:   through the IR on edges (transport)
// 4. ENFORCE: at consumption points (consumer rule)
type Dimension<T> {
  lattice: BoundedLattice<T>
  compute_at_binding: fn(AccessShape, T) -> T
  enforce: fn(T, EnforcementContext) -> Diagnostic?
}

// How a binding accesses its source value. Determines how each dimension
// computes its value at the binding site.
type AccessShape
  = FieldAccess { field: FieldRef }      // x.foo — structural projection
  | Iteration { element: FieldRef }      // element of a collection
  | Arithmetic { op: BinOp, by: Int }    // n - 1, n / 2
  | Construction                         // new value (record literal, etc.)
  | Identity                             // passed through unchanged
  | UnknownAccess                        // can't determine — fail-closed

// Where enforcement happens. Dimensions read this to decide
// whether to gate (compile error), optimize (emit decision),
// or report (non-blocking info).
type EnforcementContext
  = GateContext { binding_id: NodeId }   // compile-time rejection
  | EmitContext { target: String }       // emit-time optimization decision
  | ReportContext                        // non-blocking diagnostic

// A diagnostic — what the compiler produces when enforcement fails.
type Diagnostic {
  kind: DiagnosticKind
  span: Span
  message: String
  fix: SuggestedFix?
}

type DiagnosticKind = Error | Warning | Info

// The thesis: "show the correct code." A diagnostic carries a fix
// because the compiler knows the finite set of valid corrections.
type SuggestedFix {
  description: String
  replacement: String
  span: Span
}


// =========================================================================
// BUILT-IN DIMENSION 1: PROVENANCE (termination)
//
// SubValueRelation IS the provenance dimension. It's already defined
// in Layer 6. The lattice:
//   bottom = Unknown (fail-closed, no information)
//   top    = StrictSubValue (provably smaller)
//   meet   = meet_svr (conservative merge)
//
// Binding-site computation: derive SVR from the access shape.
// Enforcement: every recursive call must show structural descent.
//
// This dimension is what CX reads. When provenance flows on every edge,
// the 340 CX violations → 0 because the heuristic classify_* functions
// are replaced by reading binding.provenance.
// =========================================================================

fn provenance_from_access(access: AccessShape, source: SubValueRelation) -> SubValueRelation {
  match access {
    FieldAccess { field: f } => StrictSubValue { field: f, factor: UnitShrink }
    Iteration { element: f } => IteratedSubValue { field: f }
    Arithmetic { op: _, by: k } => match source {
      PreservedValue => ArithmeticDescent { param: ParamRef { func_id: 0, index: 0 }, factor: ConstantShrink { amount: k } }
      _ => Unknown
    }
    Construction => Unknown
    Identity => source
    UnknownAccess => Unknown
  }
}


// =========================================================================
// BUILT-IN DIMENSION 2: OWNERSHIP
//
// Who can use a value, and how. Prevents aliased mutation in emitted code.
//
// Lattice:
//   bottom = Shared  (most restrictive — multiple readers, no mutation)
//   top    = Owned   (most permissive — sole owner, can move/mutate)
//   meet   = min     (more-shared wins at join points)
//
// Binding-site computation: derive from SVR (see dimension table).
// Enforcement: fan-out > 1 without last-use identification → SharedError.
//
// This replaces v2's separate ownership pass (ownership.dag, 635 lines)
// with a dimension computed during inference like everything else.
// =========================================================================

type OwnershipKind = Owned | Borrowed | Shared

fn ownership_meet(a: OwnershipKind, b: OwnershipKind) -> OwnershipKind {
  match a {
    Shared => Shared
    Borrowed => match b {
      Shared => Shared
      _ => Borrowed
    }
    Owned => b
  }
}

fn ownership_from_svr(svr: SubValueRelation) -> OwnershipKind {
  match svr {
    StrictSubValue { field: _, factor: _ } => Borrowed
    IteratedSubValue { field: _ } => Owned
    ArithmeticDescent { param: _, factor: _ } => Owned
    PreservedValue => Owned
    Unknown => Shared
  }
}


// =========================================================================
// BUILT-IN DIMENSION 3: EFFECTS
//
// What external interactions a computation may perform. Enables the
// compiler to prove idempotency, purity, and safe parallelism.
//
// Lattice:
//   bottom = Pure           (no effects — safe to parallelize, memoize)
//   top    = WritesExternal (most effectful — constrains ordering)
//   meet   = max            (more-effectful wins at join points)
//
// Binding-site computation: function calls mark effectful; pure code
// stays pure; composition via monoid (sequential) or lattice (branching).
//
// Enforcement: effectful code can't appear in a pure context.
// Idempotency = all effects are lattice meets (upsert/delete, not append).
//
// Authority: effects.dag, behavioral.dag (std/)
// =========================================================================

type EffectLevel
  = Pure              // no effects — safe to parallelize, memoize, reorder
  | ReadsLocal        // reads local state (no external I/O)
  | WritesLocal       // writes local state (accumulator mutation)
  | ReadsExternal     // reads external state (file read, HTTP GET)
  | WritesExternal    // writes external state (file write, HTTP POST)

fn effect_meet(a: EffectLevel, b: EffectLevel) -> EffectLevel {
  match a {
    WritesExternal => WritesExternal
    ReadsExternal => match b {
      WritesExternal => WritesExternal
      _ => ReadsExternal
    }
    WritesLocal => match b {
      WritesExternal => WritesExternal
      ReadsExternal => ReadsExternal
      _ => WritesLocal
    }
    ReadsLocal => match b {
      Pure => ReadsLocal
      _ => b
    }
    Pure => b
  }
}

// Effect composition for sequential operations (monoid).
// Sequential composition takes the max — the combined effect is at least
// as effectful as the most effectful sub-operation.
fn compose_effects(a: EffectLevel, b: EffectLevel) -> EffectLevel {
  effect_meet(a: a, b: b)
}

// Effect algebra: classify effect shapes for idempotency analysis.
// A lattice-meet effect (upsert, delete) is idempotent.
// A monoid-append effect (create, log) is not.
type EffectShape
  = LatticeEffect       // upsert, delete — idempotent (f . f = f)
  | MonoidEffect         // append, create — not idempotent
  | IdentityEffect       // read, no-op — trivially idempotent

fn is_idempotent(shape: EffectShape) -> Bool {
  match shape {
    LatticeEffect => true
    IdentityEffect => true
    MonoidEffect => false
  }
}


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 8: PROOF STRATEGIES
//
// The compiler constructs proofs. It doesn't know WHAT it's proving — it
// reads proof strategies from std/ and executes them generically.
//
// A proof strategy is three things:
//   1. A GRAPH to traverse (call graph, use-site graph, workflow graph)
//   2. A COMPOSITION ALGEBRA to apply along edges (lattice, monoid)
//   3. A GATE to check (does the composed value satisfy the property?)
//
// Execution: fold over the graph, compose with the algebra, check gate.
// One mechanism. The compiler calls it — doesn't contain it.
//
// This is the architectural bet: if this mechanism works for termination,
// ownership, and effects, it works for any dimension — including
// user-defined ones (security classification, regulatory compliance, etc.).
//
// Authority: compiler-ideal-vs-actual.md, THESIS.md §Concept unification
// Depends on: Layer 3 (self-reference/cycles), Layer 4 (BoundedLattice, Monoid),
//             Layer 6 (SVR), Layer 7 (dimensions)
// =========================================================================

// A graph to traverse. The proof mechanism doesn't know what nodes and
// edges mean — it just walks them and composes.
type GraphTraversal<N, E> {
  nodes: List<N>
  edges_from: fn(N) -> List<GraphEdge<N, E>>
}

type GraphEdge<N, E> {
  target: N
  evidence: E
}

// A proof strategy: traverse + compose + gate.
// Parameterized by:
//   N = node type in the graph (e.g., function id for call graph)
//   E = evidence type on edges (e.g., DescentEvidence)
//   R = result type (e.g., TerminationProof)
type ProofStrategy<N, E, R> {
  graph: GraphTraversal<N, E>
  compose: fn(E, E) -> E            // algebra: how evidence combines
  identity: E                        // algebra: identity element
  gate: fn(N, E) -> ProofResult<R>  // check: is composed evidence sufficient?
}

type ProofResult<R>
  = ProofSuccess { proof: R }
  | ProofFailure { diagnostic: Diagnostic }

// Execute a proof strategy: fold over SCCs, compose evidence along
// cycle edges, check gate on each SCC.
//
// The key insight: SCC decomposition + fold is the universal proof
// structure. Every cycle in the graph must satisfy the gate. Acyclic
// portions are trivially satisfied (no cycle = no descent needed).
//
// fn execute_proof<N, E, R>(strategy: ProofStrategy<N, E, R>) -> List<ProofResult<R>> {
//   let sccs = tarjan_scc(strategy.graph)
//   sccs |> map(scc =>
//     let composed = fold(
//       scc.cycle_edges,
//       init: strategy.identity,
//       f: (acc, edge) => strategy.compose(acc, edge.evidence)
//     )
//     strategy.gate(scc.representative, composed)
//   )
// }
//
// This is pseudocode — the actual implementation uses whatever SCC
// algorithm the target language provides. The point: the compiler
// contains ONE generic proof executor, not per-dimension proof code.


// =========================================================================
// PROOF STRATEGY 1: TERMINATION
//
// Every recursive function (or SCC of mutually recursive functions)
// must terminate. Proof: at least one ranking dimension strictly
// decreases across every cycle in the call graph.
//
// graph:   call graph decomposed into SCCs
// compose: merge descent evidence (lattice meet across branches)
// gate:    every cycle has lexicographic descent
//
// Authority: termination.dag (std/)
// =========================================================================

// Descent evidence at a single call edge.
type DescentEvidence
  = Strict           // value provably decreases (Less)
  | NonIncreasing    // value does not increase (Equal or Less)
  | DescentUnknown   // cannot determine (fail-closed)

fn merge_descent(a: DescentEvidence, b: DescentEvidence) -> DescentEvidence {
  match a {
    Strict => match b {
      Strict => Strict
      NonIncreasing => NonIncreasing
      DescentUnknown => DescentUnknown
    }
    NonIncreasing => match b {
      Strict => NonIncreasing
      NonIncreasing => NonIncreasing
      DescentUnknown => DescentUnknown
    }
    DescentUnknown => DescentUnknown
  }
}

// What is decreasing — the ranking dimension.
type RankingDimension
  = TreeSize { param: ParamRef }         // |subtree| via child accessor
  | ListLength { param: ParamRef }       // |list| via skip/tail
  | ArithmeticValue { param: ParamRef }  // integer via n-1, n/2
  | TokenPosition { param: ParamRef }    // parser position
  | SetCardinality { param: ParamRef }   // |set| via remove

// A termination proof: lexicographic ordering over ranking dimensions.
// At each recursive call edge, the FIRST dimension that changes must
// strictly decrease. Higher dimensions must not increase.
type TerminationProof {
  dimensions: List<RankingDimension>
}

// Evidence at one call edge: how each dimension changes.
type CallEdgeEvidence {
  caller: NodeId
  callee: NodeId
  per_dimension: List<DescentEvidence>
}

// The gate: is the proof valid? Every cycle in the SCC must have
// lexicographic descent — at least one dimension strictly decreases
// while all higher dimensions are non-increasing.
fn is_lexicographic_descent(evidence: List<DescentEvidence>) -> Bool {
  // Walk dimensions in order. First Strict found = descent proven.
  // Any DescentUnknown before a Strict = proof fails.
  fold(evidence, init: false, f: (found_strict, e) =>
    if found_strict { true }
    else {
      match e {
        Strict => true
        NonIncreasing => false
        DescentUnknown => false
      }
    }
  )
}


// =========================================================================
// PROOF STRATEGY 2: OWNERSHIP
//
// No aliased mutation in emitted code. For each binding: if fan-out > 1
// (multiple consumers), the binding must be Shared (cloned) or the
// compiler must identify the last use (move semantics).
//
// graph:   use-site graph (per-binding: which expressions reference it)
// compose: ownership_meet (more-shared wins)
// gate:    no binding has fan-out > 1 with Owned kind (would be aliased)
//
// Authority: binding-model-proposal.md, ownership-design.md
// =========================================================================

type OwnershipProof {
  binding_id: NodeId
  kind: OwnershipKind
  fan_out: Int
  last_use: NodeId?
}

type OwnershipResult
  = SoleOwner                                // fan-out = 1: move
  | LastUseIdentified { last: NodeId }       // fan-out > 1 but last use known: move last, clone rest
  | SharedOwnership                          // fan-out > 1, no last use: clone all

fn check_ownership(binding_id: NodeId, kind: OwnershipKind, fan_out: Int, last_use: NodeId?) -> OwnershipResult {
  if fan_out <= 1 { SoleOwner }
  else {
    match last_use {
      Some { value: last } => LastUseIdentified { last: last }
      None => SharedOwnership
    }
  }
}


// =========================================================================
// PROOF STRATEGY 3: EFFECTS (idempotency + safety)
//
// Workflow composition: is the composed effect of a sequence of operations
// idempotent? Safe to parallelize? Safe to retry?
//
// graph:   workflow DAG (operations connected by data flow)
// compose: compose_effects (monoid: sequential max)
// gate:    composed effect satisfies declared property (idempotent, pure, etc.)
//
// Authority: effects.dag (std/)
// =========================================================================

type WorkflowProof {
  workflow_id: NodeId
  composed_effect: EffectLevel
  composed_shape: EffectShape
  is_idempotent: Bool
}

// Compose effect shapes across a workflow.
// Lattice effects compose to lattice. Monoid poisons the chain.
// Identity is absorbed.
fn compose_shapes(a: EffectShape, b: EffectShape) -> EffectShape {
  match a {
    MonoidEffect => MonoidEffect
    LatticeEffect => match b {
      MonoidEffect => MonoidEffect
      _ => LatticeEffect
    }
    IdentityEffect => b
  }
}

// The gate: does the workflow satisfy its declared property?
fn check_workflow_effect(composed: EffectShape, declared_idempotent: Bool) -> ProofResult<WorkflowProof> {
  if declared_idempotent && !is_idempotent(shape: composed) {
    ProofFailure { diagnostic: Diagnostic {
      kind: Error,
      span: Span { file: "", start: 0, end: 0 },
      message: "workflow declared idempotent but contains non-idempotent operation",
      fix: none
    }}
  } else {
    ProofSuccess { proof: WorkflowProof {
      workflow_id: 0,
      composed_effect: Pure,
      composed_shape: composed,
      is_idempotent: is_idempotent(shape: composed)
    }}
  }
}


// =========================================================================
// LAYER 9: PIPELINE CONTRACTS
//
// Four stages. Each a pure function with a typed contract.
// The pipeline is a composition: emit(prove(resolve(parse(source)))).
//
//   Source → PARSE → SurfaceAST → RESOLVE → ModuleGraph → PROVE → ProvenGraph → EMIT → Files
//
// Key insight: ProvenGraph = ModuleGraph where every edge carries
// dimension values. The prove stage ATTACHES them. The emit stage
// only READS them. No reconstruction, no heuristics, no second pass.
//
// Authority: DESIGN.md (src/v2/), compiler-ideal-vs-actual.md
// =========================================================================


// ---- STAGE 1: PARSE ----
// Input:  source text
// Output: surface AST (Design B typed nodes) + diagnostics
//
// The parser produces precisely typed nodes (Expr, Decl, Pattern).
// No name resolution, no type information, no SVR — just structure
// with source spans. Parse errors are diagnostics, not exceptions.

type Source {
  path: String
  text: String
}

type ParseResult {
  module_name: String
  declarations: List<Decl>
  diagnostics: List<Diagnostic>
  intern_table: InternTable
}

// String interning: names → integer ids. Happens once during parse.
// All downstream stages use NodeId, never string comparison for identity.
type InternTable {
  strings: List<String>
  index: Map<String, Int>
}

fn intern(table: InternTable, name: String) -> InternResult {
  match map_get(table.index, name) {
    Some { value: id } => InternResult { id: id, table: table }
    None =>
      let id = table.strings |> count
      InternResult {
        id: id,
        table: InternTable {
          strings: list_push(table.strings, name),
          index: map_insert(table.index, name, id)
        }
      }
  }
}

type InternResult { id: Int, table: InternTable }


// ---- STAGE 2: RESOLVE ----
// Input:  list of parsed modules (Design B ASTs)
// Output: module graph (Design A universal nodes with resolved names)
//
// Resolve does three things:
//   1. Connect modules: imports, exports, topological sort
//   2. Resolve names: every Var gets a BindingRef pointing to its definition
//   3. Lower surface → IR: Design B (typed) → Design A (universal)
//
// After resolve, every node has a NodeId, every name is resolved to a
// binding site, and the graph is ready for dimension attachment.

type ModuleGraph {
  modules: List<ResolvedModule>
  call_graph: CallGraph
  type_env: TypeEnv
}

type ResolvedModule {
  name: String
  nodes: Map<NodeId, UnivNode>
  root_declarations: List<NodeId>
}

// Call graph: which functions call which. Needed for SCC decomposition
// in the termination proof strategy.
type CallGraph {
  edges: Map<NodeId, List<NodeId>>
}

// Type environment: resolved types for all named bindings.
type TypeEnv {
  bindings: Map<NodeId, TypeBinding>
  recursive_types: Map<NodeId, Bool>
  inductive_fields: Map<NodeId, List<InductiveField>>
}

// The binding: what a name resolves to, with its type and provenance.
// This is v2's TypeBinding with NodeId identity instead of string keys,
// and dimension values on the binding from the start.
type TypeBinding {
  id: NodeId
  resolved_type: TypeRef
  provenance: SubValueRelation
}

// Inductive field: a field whose type references the parent type.
// The compiler DISCOVERS these by comparing each field's type against
// the type being defined. No RecursionShape enum — the compiler reads
// the field's resolved type and sees the self-reference directly.
type InductiveField {
  field: FieldRef
  field_type: TypeRef
}

// Function signatures: resolved parameter types + return type.
type FuncSig {
  id: NodeId
  params: List<FuncParam>
  return_type: TypeRef
  is_async: Bool
  output_provenance: Map<String, SubValueRelation>
}

type FuncParam {
  name: String
  id: NodeId
  type_ref: TypeRef
}

type FuncEnv {
  signatures: Map<NodeId, FuncSig>
}


// ---- STAGE 3: PROVE ----
// Input:  module graph (resolved names, types, call graph)
// Output: proven graph (dimension values on EVERY edge) + proof results
//
// This is ONE stage, not three. The compiler:
//   1. Walks the resolved graph
//   2. At each binding site, computes ALL dimension values using the
//      dimension's compose function and the SVR on the edge
//   3. For each proof strategy declared in std/, executes it:
//      fold over the relevant graph, compose with the algebra, check gate
//
// The output is the same ModuleGraph, enriched with dimension values
// on every edge. Downstream stages (emit) only read — never compute.

type ProvenGraph {
  modules: List<ResolvedModule>
  call_graph: CallGraph
  type_env: TypeEnv
  func_env: FuncEnv
  // Dimension values: side tables keyed by edge identity.
  // Not on the edge struct itself — keeps the IR lean and allows
  // user-defined dimensions without changing edge types.
  provenance: Map<NodeId, SubValueRelation>
  ownership: Map<NodeId, OwnershipKind>
  effects: Map<NodeId, EffectLevel>
  // Proof results per function/workflow.
  termination_proofs: Map<NodeId, TerminationProof>
  ownership_proofs: Map<NodeId, OwnershipProof>
  workflow_proofs: Map<NodeId, WorkflowProof>
  // All diagnostics from proof construction.
  diagnostics: List<Diagnostic>
}


// ---- STAGE 4: EMIT ----
// Input:  proven graph + language spec
// Output: target language source files
//
// Emission is mechanical translation. The emitter reads:
//   - The proven graph (types, expressions, dimension values)
//   - The language spec (syntax, sharing strategy, naming conventions)
// It never decides. It translates.
//
// Adding a new target language = adding a LanguageSpec.
// Zero compiler changes. The spec IS the implementation.

type LanguageSpec {
  name: String
  file_extension: String
  // How types map to target syntax
  type_syntax: TypeSyntaxSpec
  // How expressions map to target syntax
  expr_syntax: ExprSyntaxSpec
  // How ownership maps to target representation
  sharing: SharingStrategy
  // How modules map to target file structure
  module_structure: ModuleStructureSpec
}

// How ownership decisions become target-language code.
type SharingStrategy
  = ReferenceCounted       // Rust: Rc<T>
  | GarbageCollected       // Go/Python/Java: plain T, GC handles it
  | ValueSemantics         // C: copy everything, manual free
  | BorrowChecked          // Rust with borrows: &T / &mut T / T

type TypeSyntaxSpec {
  record_syntax: String       // e.g., "struct" (Rust), "type" (Go), "class" (Python)
  enum_syntax: String         // e.g., "enum" (Rust), "type ... interface" (Go)
  optional_syntax: String     // e.g., "Option<T>" (Rust), "*T" (Go), "Optional[T]" (Python)
  list_syntax: String         // e.g., "Vec<T>" (Rust), "[]T" (Go), "List[T]" (Python)
  map_syntax: String          // e.g., "HashMap<K,V>", "map[K]V", "Dict[K,V]"
}

type ExprSyntaxSpec {
  call_syntax: String         // e.g., "func(args)" vs "func args"
  match_syntax: String        // e.g., "match" (Rust), "switch" (Go)
  lambda_syntax: String       // e.g., "|x| x" (Rust), "func(x)" (Go), "lambda x:" (Python)
  block_syntax: String        // e.g., "{ ... }" vs indentation
}

type ModuleStructureSpec {
  one_file_per: String        // "module" | "type" | "package"
  import_syntax: String       // e.g., "use", "import"
}

// The output: target language source files.
type TargetFile {
  path: String
  content: String
  language: String
}

type EmitResult {
  files: List<TargetFile>
  diagnostics: List<Diagnostic>
}


// =========================================================================
// THE FULL PIPELINE
//
// A compiler is the composition of these four stages:
//
//   fn compile(sources: List<Source>, target: LanguageSpec) -> EmitResult {
//     let parsed = sources |> map(s => parse(source: s))
//     let resolved = resolve(modules: parsed)
//     let proven = prove(graph: resolved)
//     emit(graph: proven, spec: target)
//   }
//
// Each stage is pure. No mutation, no global state. The pipeline is
// a fold over stages with the graph as the accumulator.
//
// Error handling: every stage returns diagnostics. The pipeline threads
// them. If any stage produces blocking diagnostics (Error kind), the
// pipeline can stop early or continue for maximum error reporting.
// =========================================================================


// =========================================================================
// SPEC SUMMARY
//
// 10 layers, each derived from prior layers:
//
//   0  Truth + Finiteness    — Classical, max_inhabitants
//   1  Function              — A → B (the sole computation primitive)
//   2  Type structure         — product/coproduct/unit/void EMERGE from syntax
//   3  Self-reference        — cycles in type/call graphs (no declared types)
//   4  Observed patterns     — BoundedLattice, Monoid, Ordering
//   5  The DAG IR            — Node, Edge (Designs A and B)
//   6  Edge vocabulary       — SubValueRelation, ShrinkFactor, UsageEdge
//   7  Dimension mechanism   — Dimension<T>, provenance, ownership, effects
//   8  Proof strategies      — ProofStrategy, termination, ownership, effects
//   9  Pipeline contracts    — parse / resolve / prove / emit
//
// The derivation order encodes the thesis:
//   - Truth is primitive; function is the sole computation primitive.
//   - Product/coproduct are not declared types — they are structural
//     shapes that EMERGE from how type definitions compose truth values.
//   - Recursion is not a declared concept — it is a cycle in the graph
//     that the compiler detects.
//   - Algebraic patterns (lattice, monoid) are not foundations — they
//     are regularities OBSERVED in finite structures. The compiler
//     discovers them in std/ declarations, not as built-in knowledge.
//   - User-defined dimensions work identically to built-in ones
//     because both are instances of the same Layer 4 patterns.
//   - Emission to ANY target (Rust, Verilog, SQL, SPICE, English)
//     works because the composition operators are universal arithmetic.
//
// vs v2:
//   32 .dag files, 38,078 lines, 18-field Node, 22-variant ExprData,
//   Connective enum, VarBindingKind, separate ownership pass,
//   triple classification system, string-keyed identity
//
// What this spec eliminates:
//   - Node.name (string identity → NodeId)
//   - Connective enum (→ structural shape visible in type definition syntax)
//   - VarBindingKind (→ edge position: parameter vs body)
//   - Separate ownership pass (→ dimension on bindings)
//   - classify_binding_provenance, classify_let_value,
//     classify_argument, classify_body_provenance,
//     classify_self_call_evidence (→ read SVR from edge)
//   - Per-dimension proof code (→ generic ProofStrategy executor)
//   - String-keyed tables (→ NodeId-keyed tables)
//   - Algebraic axioms in Layer 0 (→ observed patterns in Layer 4)
//   - RecursionShape enum (→ compiler observes self-reference in type graph)
//   - IterationPrimitive enum (→ compiler classifies call graph shapes)
//   - TypeShape enum (→ structural shape visible in definition syntax)
//
// What this spec preserves:
//   - SubValueRelation as the edge vocabulary (from std/induction.dag)
//   - BoundedLattice as the dimension carrier (from std/algebra.dag)
//   - fold + lattice + gate as the proof mechanism
//   - TerminationProof with lexicographic ordering (from std/termination.dag)
//   - LanguageSpec-driven emission
//   - The thesis: if it compiles, the intent is sound
//
// Open questions for implementation:
//   1. Design A vs Design B (or hybrid: B for parse, A for prove/emit)
//   2. Dimension table storage: side tables vs edge fields
//   3. User-defined dimensions: how they declare composition rules
//   4. How prove stage discovers strategies from std/ declarations
//   5. Interpreter: same ProvenGraph, tree-walk evaluation instead of emit
// =========================================================================

